#{[17592186364206 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=49$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{7\\sqrt{x}-x} & =\\frac{49-49}{7\\sqrt{49}-49}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{7\\sqrt{x}-x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(x-49)(7\\sqrt{x}+x)}{(7\\sqrt{x}-x)(7\\sqrt{x}+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(x-49)(7\\sqrt{x}+x)}{{(7\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(x-49)(7\\sqrt{x}+x)}{49x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(x-49)(7\\sqrt{x}+x)}{-x(x-49)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{7\\sqrt{x}+x}{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{49}+49}{-49} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186364282 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 81}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-9}{x-81} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{81}-9}{81-81}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 81}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-9}{x-81} & =\\lim_{x\\to 81}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-9)(\\sqrt{x}+9)}{(x-81)(\\sqrt{x}+9)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 81}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{9}^{2}}{(x-81)(\\sqrt{x}+9)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 81}\\frac{x-81}{(x-81)(\\sqrt{x}+9)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 81}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{81}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186364289 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=27$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 27}\\frac{x-27}{\\sqrt{3x}-9} & =\\frac{27-27}{\\sqrt{3(27)}-9}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 27}\\frac{x-27}{\\sqrt{3x}-9} & =\\lim_{x\\to 27}\\frac{(x-27)(\\sqrt{3x}+9)}{(\\sqrt{3x}-9)(\\sqrt{3x}+9)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 27}\\frac{(x-27)(\\sqrt{3x}+9)}{{(\\sqrt{3x})}^{2}-{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 27}\\frac{(x-27)(\\sqrt{3x}+9)}{3x-81} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 27}\\frac{(x-27)(\\sqrt{3x}+9)}{3(x-27)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 27}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x}+9}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(27)}+9}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+9}{3} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186364859 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}-x-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{x}^{2}]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}-x-1]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{x}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-1+{e}^{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{x}-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[2x]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}-1]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365568 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}+9x-1}{3x+5} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\sqrt{x}+9x-1]^{′}}{[3x+5]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}+9)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365571 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{m}=a+mΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1),$ the lower bound is $a=2$ and the upper bound is $b=8.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{8-2}{n}=\\frac{6}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(2+m\\cdot \\frac{6}{n})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(2+\\frac{6m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5(2+\\frac{6m}{n})+1)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11+\\frac{30m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365797 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]}^{′}}{{[{e}^{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{-{csc}^{2}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{-{csc}^{2}⁡x}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}\\cancel{(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367040 :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{2}, & x=4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}, & x=5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}, & x=6\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$"] [17592186367047 :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{4}, & x=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}, & x=3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{12}, & x=4\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$"] [17592186367205 :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}0.25, & x=10 \\\\ 0.4, & x=20 \\\\ 0.35, & x=30\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$"] [17592186367247 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=(\\lim_{t\\to 0}(1-{t}^{2}))i+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1}{t-1})j+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\sqrt{t-1})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}(1-{t}^{2}) & =1-{0}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{0-1}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\sqrt{1-t} & =\\sqrt{1-0}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=i-j+k.$"] [17592186367261 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }f(t),$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\pi }{t} \\\\ \\frac{2t}{\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t)}{\\pi -t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186367261 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\pi }{t} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{2t}{\\pi } \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t)}{\\pi -t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\pi }{t} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\pi }{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{0-\\pi }{\\pi } \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{2t}{\\pi } & =\\frac{2\\pi }{\\pi }=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t)}{\\pi -t} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t))}^{′}}{(\\pi -t)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi -t)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi -\\pi )}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186367268 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }‖f(t)‖,$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\pi }{t} \\\\ \\frac{2t}{\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t)}{\\pi -t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186367268 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\pi }{t} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{2t}{\\pi } \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t)}{\\pi -t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\pi }{t} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\pi }{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{0-\\pi }{\\pi } \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{2t}{\\pi } & =\\frac{2\\pi }{\\pi }=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t)}{\\pi -t} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t))}^{′}}{(\\pi -t)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi -t)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi -\\pi )}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367275 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨\\lim_{s\\to 2}{e}^{s-2},\\,\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-s},\\,\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\frac{3}{s+2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}{e}^{s-2} & ={e}^{2-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-s} & =\\sqrt{6-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\frac{3}{s+2} & =\\frac{3}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨1,2,\\frac{3}{4}⟩.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}‖f(s)‖ & =‖\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖⟨1,2,\\frac{3}{4}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{89}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367282 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,\\lim_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5)⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}} & =\\frac{5}{{e}^{0}}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) & =3\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2(0)) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5) & ={2}^{3}-5=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨5,3,3⟩.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}‖f(s)‖ & =‖\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖⟨5,3,3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+9+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{43}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367289 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨6-{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}-6,\\,\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t),\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367289 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,(6-{t}^{3}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,({t}^{2}-6),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6-{2}^{3},{2}^{2}-6,\\frac{2}{{2}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,\\frac{1}{4}⟩ \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 2}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,(5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2),\\sqrt{6-2},2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,\\frac{1}{4}⟩+⟨5,2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2+5,-2+2,\\frac{1}{4}+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,\\frac{17}{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367296 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}[4f(t)-7g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={t}^{3}\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+(3-t)\\,j-({t}^{2}-1)k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367296 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}f(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}{t}^{3})i+(\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}{t}^{2})j+(\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2))k \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}\\,i+(-1)^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1+2)\\,k \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}g(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}\\frac{2}{t})i+(\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}(3-t))j-(\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}({t}^{2}-1))k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)}\\,i+(3-(-1))\\,j-((-1)^{2}-1)k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}[4f(t)-7g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}4f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}7g(t) \\\\  & =4\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}f(t)-7\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}g(t) \\\\  & =4(-i+j)-7(-2\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-4\\,i+4\\,j+14\\,i-28\\,j \\\\  & =10\\,i-24\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367303 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}[3f(t)-2g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+t \\\\ {e}^{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)}{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{1-2t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367303 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}({t}^{2}+t) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}{e}^{t+1} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)}{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{2}+(-1) \\\\ {e}^{-1+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-1+1)}{(-1)}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}2 \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}\\frac{1-2t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1+1) \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{1-2(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}[3f(t)-2g(t)] & =3\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}f(t)-2\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}g(t) \\\\  & =3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367310 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+t \\\\ {e}^{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)}{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{1-2t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367310 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}({t}^{2}+t) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}{e}^{t+1} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)}{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{2}+(-1) \\\\ {e}^{-1+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-1+1)}{(-1)}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}2 \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}\\frac{1-2t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1+1) \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{1-2(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to \\,-1}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0(0)+1(2)+(-1)\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367317 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{1-t}⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨2t-{t}^{2},\\,2t-1,\\,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367317 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,t,\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}{e}^{1-t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,{1}^{2},{e}^{1-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,(2t-{t}^{2}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,(2t-1),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1)-{1}^{2},2(1)-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =1(1)+1(1)+1(3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367568 :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{5}{18}, & x=0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=2\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$"] [17592186367978 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\times g(t)]$ given that\n$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{t-1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t+8 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{t}^{2}+3} \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367978 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 1}({t}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{t-1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 1}(t+8) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}+3} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}11\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+8 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{1}^{2}+3} \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the change of variable $u=t-1$ and the property $\\lim_{u\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡u}{u}=1.$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡1 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 11\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 11\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡1 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1\\cdot 11-1\\cdot 1)\\,i-(0\\cdot 11-1\\cdot 9)\\,j+(0\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡1\\cdot 9)\\,k \\\\  & =(11\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-1)\\,i+9\\,j-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡1k \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367985 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =⟨\\lim_{s\\to 0}{e}^{s},\\,\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{s}^{2}-2}{s+6},\\,\\lim_{s\\to 0}{4}^{s}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}{e}^{s} & ={e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{s}^{2}-2}{s+6} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-2}{0+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}{4}^{s} & ={4}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{3},\\,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{3},\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{19}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367992 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)=⟨\\lim_{s\\to 0}(4{s}^{7}+13),\\,\\lim_{s\\to 0}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{s}^{2}+13}{{e}^{s}}⟩\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}(4{s}^{7}+13) & =4(0)^{7}+13=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡s & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{s}^{2}+13}{{e}^{s}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}+13}{{e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{1} \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)=⟨13,0,13⟩.$"] [17592186368296 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-7)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<7 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 7\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(t-7){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{7}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{7}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{7}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{7}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{7}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{7}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-7s}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{s}(0-{e}^{-7s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-7s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{{e}^{-7s}}{s},\\,s>0.\n$$"] [17592186368315 :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{-t},\\, & t\\le 4 \\\\ 2\\, & t>4.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}."] [17592186368315 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{-t},\\, & t\\le 4 \\\\ 2\\, & t>4\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-(1+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-(1+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-(1+s)}{[{e}^{-(1+s)t}]}_{0}^{4}+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-4(1+s)}}{1+s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-4(1+s)}}{1+s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-2{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-4(1+s)}}{1+s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-4(1+s)}}{1+s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-4(1+s)}}{1+s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-4s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"] [17592186368641 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 1}[4f(t)-g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+(4-t)\\,j-({t}^{2}-1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368641 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 1}{t}^{2})\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 1}t)\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =({1}^{2})\\,i+1\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i+1\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =i+j, \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{3}{t})\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 1}(4-t))\\,j-(\\lim_{t\\to 1}({t}^{2}-1))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\,i+(4-1)\\,j-({1}^{2}-1)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+3\\,j-0\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+3\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[4f(t)-g(t)] & =4\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =4(i+j)-(3\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368648 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[2f(t)+3g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={t}^{2}\\,i+(1+t)\\,j-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,i-{e}^{t}\\,j+(t-1)^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368648 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 0}{t}^{2})\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}(1+t))\\,j-(\\lim_{t\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\,k \\\\  & =({0}^{2})\\,i+(1+0)\\,j-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+1\\,j-0\\,k \\\\  & =j, \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 0}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1))\\,i-(\\lim_{t\\to 0}{e}^{t})\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t-1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-1)\\,i-{e}^{0}\\,j+(0-1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i-1\\,j+1\\,k \\\\  & =-j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[2f(t)+3g(t)] & =2\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)+3\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =2(j)+3(-j+k) \\\\  & =-j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368655 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\times g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{t}\\,j+({t}^{2}+3)\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\,j+(t-2)^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368655 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\sqrt{t})\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}+3))\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,i+\\sqrt{0}\\,j+({0}^{2}+3)\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i+0\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,k \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1))\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t})\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t-2)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)\\,i+1\\,j+(0-2)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+1\\,j+4\\,k \\\\  & =j+4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second component of $\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }g(t)$ is a special limit.\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,k)\\times (j+4\\,k) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3)\\,i-(1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3)\\,j+(1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0)\\,k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+1\\,k \\\\  & =-3i-4\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368662 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨{e}^{-t},\\,{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t},\\,4⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}},\\,\\frac{t+1}{t},\\,{t}^{-5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368662 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,e,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second component of $\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t)$ is a special limit.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}},\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{t+1}{t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-5}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-1)},\\,1+\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{t}^{5}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,e,\\,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)+e\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368730 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{-4t}-1}{t} \\\\ \\frac{2{t}^{2}-8}{t-1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186368730 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{-4t}-1}{t} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2{t}^{2}-8}{t-1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- The first component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t} & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t})\\cdot (\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}\\right) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{-4t}-1}{t} & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{({e}^{-4t}-1)^{′}}{(t)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{-4{e}^{-4t}}{1} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{0} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2{t}^{2}-8}{t-1} & =\\frac{2(0)^{2}-8}{(0)-1}=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+16+64} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368962 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}}\\text{d}z & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\left(-\\frac{4}{z}\\right)]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{4}{a}+\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186368969 :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-5}]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2\\sqrt{6-5}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{1/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt[3]{a}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ are divergent."] [17592186369154 :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{10}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{10}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{10}{4}.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 10, & \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{4},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 10.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)"] [17592186369161 :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $14,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 7$ and $\\pm 14.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 6$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{12}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{6}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{12}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{6}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{12}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{14}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{6}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{12}.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{12}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{6}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{12}, \\\\ \\pm 14, & \\pm 7, & \\pm \\frac{14}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{6}.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{12},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{12},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{4},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{14}{3},\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 14.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $32$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 16=32.$)"] [17592186369553 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple, the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)+g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}2f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2f(1)+g(1) \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{⟨0,1,-2⟩}_{f(1)}+g(1) \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-4⟩+g(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)+g(t)]=⟨-1,4,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $g(1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)+g(t)] & =⟨-1,4,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,2,-4⟩+g(1) & =⟨-1,4,2⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨-1,4,2⟩-⟨0,2,-4⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨-1,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369560 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and the addition rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 4}[3f(t)-2g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 4}\\text{ }3f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to 4}2g(t) \\\\  & =3\\lim_{t\\to 4}f(t)-2\\lim_{t\\to 4}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\lim_{t\\to 4}f(t)-2\\lim_{t\\to 4}g(t) & =3f(4)-2g(4) \\\\  & =3⟨1,2,-2⟩-2⟨0,2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-6⟩-⟨0,4,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369818 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(1+{x}^{3})}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(1+{x}^{3})}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369825 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\infty }^{2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(0-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369832 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{t}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369964 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3a+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3a+1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."] [17592186369971 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\infty }^{2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369978 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{(-3)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{27}+\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{27}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186370621 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(x+1)^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(x+1)^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{7}{u}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{7/2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{7/2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186370627 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =16\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =16\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-2})]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-8(0-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186370630 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{1/2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{1/2}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186370633 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{7}-3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7{x}^{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=7{x}^{6}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{7}-3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-4$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{({x}^{7}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371014 :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[u(t)g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}u(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =u(1)g(1) \\\\  & =5g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 1}[u(t)g(t)]=⟨5,-10,20⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $g(1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[u(t)g(t)] & =⟨5,-10,20⟩ \\\\ 5g(1) & =⟨5,-10,2⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨5,-10,20⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨1,-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371028 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and the addition rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to -2}[\\frac{f(x)}{3}+2g(x)] & =\\lim_{t\\to -2}\\frac{f(x)}{3}+\\lim_{t\\to -2}2g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{t\\to -2}f(t)+2\\lim_{t\\to -2}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{t\\to -2}f(t)+2\\lim_{t\\to -2}g(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}f(-2)+2g(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,3,-9⟩+2⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-3⟩+⟨4,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186375031 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=5.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(5) & =\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)=-3(5)=-15 \\\\ {f}^{+}(5) & =\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={5}^{2}-26=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(5)+{f}^{+}(5)}{2}=\\frac{-15+(-1)}{2}=-8.\n$$"] [17592186375034 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{0+2}{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186375055 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=2 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{2+(-4)}{2}=-1.\n$$"] [17592186376075 :question/problem "![](images/52/52cb038e475b4d0d5de64684340e7291e0c8cd82072f2f30a1c4025af69cc809.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}2x,\\, & 0\\le x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186376552 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n∀ε>0,\\,∃N\\in N,∀n\\in N,\\,n\\ge N\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε.\n$$\n\nLet's write this in words.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $ε>0,$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε.\n$$\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $ε.$\n\nFirst, simplifying $|{a}_{n}-L|,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}+\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3(1-{n}^{2})}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}+\\frac{3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3-3{n}^{2}+3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}>0$ for any natural $n\\ge 2.$ With that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n|{a}_{n}-L|=|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|=\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $|{a}_{n}-L|<ε$ for $n\\ge 2:$\n\nSolving this inequality for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} & <ε \\\\ 4 & <ε(9{n}^{2}-21) \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε} & <9{n}^{2}-21 \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε}+21 & <9{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9ε}+\\frac{7}{3} & <{n}^{2} \\\\ n & >\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want $$\n\\displaystyle |\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε,\n$$ it's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\nTherefore, we choose $N>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nNotice that, since $\\frac{4}{9ε}>0,$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}≈1.53\n$$ and therefore $N\\ge 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\nThen, for any natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)| & =\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} \\\\  & <\\frac{4}{9{(\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}})}^{2}-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(21+\\frac{4}{ε}-21)} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\nTherefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186377351 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-x+3, & x<2, \\\\ 2, & x\\ge 2.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186377354 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-\\frac{3}{2}x-1, & x<2, \\\\ x-4, & x\\ge 2.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186377357 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}, & x<2, \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}x+4, & x\\ge 2.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186377360 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}-1, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{4}{x}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{{e}^{3}}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186377535 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{5}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{5}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{10{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{5}+1)^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{u}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{10{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{5}+1)^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{u}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }2{u}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}2{u}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{4}{7}{u}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{7/2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{7/2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186377695 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)-(-3)}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{(x-1)y}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-3.$"] [17592186377708 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=2x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186378477 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{({n}^{2}+2)({n}^{2}-2)}{{n}^{2}(3-n)}=\\frac{{n}^{4}-4}{3{n}^{2}-{n}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}-4}{3{n}^{2}-{n}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}-4}{3{n}^{2}-{n}^{3}} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{4}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{4}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n-\\frac{4}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3}{n}-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n-0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence diverges."] [17592186378622 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,\\pi )$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186378629 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}-0(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"] [17592186378636 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186379328 :question/worked-solution "The dominant term in the denominator is $8{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{n}^{6}+4{n}^{5}}{8{n}^{4}+10} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8{n}^{6}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{4{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{8{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{10}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{n}^{2}+4n}{(8+\\frac{10}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{n}^{2}+4n}{8+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }({n}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}n) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges."] [17592186379716 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(5n-3)(5n+3)}{n(2-25n)}=\\frac{25{n}^{2}-9}{2n-25{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{25{n}^{2}-9}{2n-25{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-25{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{25{n}^{2}-9}{2n-25{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{25{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{25{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(25-\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2}{n}-25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-0}{0-25} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$"] [17592186380041 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the expression under the radical is negative. However, the point $(3,1,-1)$ does not cause the  expression under the radical  to be negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-3,1,-1)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z} & =\\sqrt{3-1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-1-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186380048 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1,-2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-2)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot (-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2}{1+1-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186380055 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0\\cdot 2}{{0}^{3}-2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x(x-y)}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2} & =\\frac{0-2}{0+2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"] [17592186380062 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $1$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186380242 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"] [17592186380824 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-{x}^{2}+2$ and $y=x.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{3}-xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{cases}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({x}^{3}-xy)\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186380824 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/3f/3f071c27bfa281abcaabe63d4d67797ea7b051b931ee7658d2a32a58f80a0bac.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{3}-xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nthen we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{3}-xy)\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,1]$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles $R=[-2,1]\\times [-2,2]$ and $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$ cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[-2,1]\\times [-2,2]$; $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$ only."] [17592186380831 :question/problem "![](images/c5/c54c756568a5f20d1d82a228c333dc4253a82236060ab2a7aea6260b10612140.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}x{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186380831 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}x{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-4,3]\\times [-2,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,4]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-4,3]\\times [-2,3]$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,4]$” only."] [17592186380908 :question/problem "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<0,\\,1<y<3, \\\\ 2\\sqrt{xy},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<2,\\,1<y<3.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(-1,2)\\times (1,3)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186381881 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4{x}^{2}-2}{{x}^{5}+8} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-x+\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(1+\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{-x+0-0}{1+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,-x \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186381884 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7}{-5{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}\\right)}{(\\frac{-5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}\\right)}{(-5+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-5+0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"] [17592186382193 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{2}x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,-2<x<0,\\,1<y<3, \\\\ \\frac{x}{y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<2,\\,1<y<3,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [17592186383487 :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{5}{12}, & x=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}, & x=2\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{5}].$"] [17592186386717 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x},$ the lower bound is $a=0,$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{n}=\\frac{3}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f\\left(\\frac{3i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{3i/n}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386730 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f\\left(\\frac{3k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390528 :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $49,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 7$ and $\\pm 49.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{49}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{49}.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{49}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{49},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 7.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $8$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 4=8.$)"] [17592186390841 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to -2}f(s)=⟨\\lim_{s\\to -2}(s+3)^{1/(s+2)},\\,\\lim_{s\\to -2}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(s+2)}{s+2},\\,\\lim_{s\\to -2}\\frac{{s}^{2}+2s}{{s}^{2}+3s+2}⟩\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- The first component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to -2}(s+3)^{1/(s+2)} & =\\lim_{s\\to -2}(1+(s+2))^{1/(s+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{u\\to 0}(1+u)^{1/u} \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to -2}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(s+2)}{s+2} & =\\lim_{u\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡u}{u}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to -2}\\frac{{s}^{2}+2s}{{s}^{2}+3s+2} & =\\lim_{s\\to -2}\\frac{s(s+2)}{(s+1)(s+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{s\\to -2}\\frac{s}{s+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{-2+1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{s\\to -2}f(s)=⟨e,1,2⟩.$"] [17592186392101 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
